.TH MU2EDAQ_COMMANDLINE_TOOLS 3 "2026-07-27" "mu2edaq-commandline-tools 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Python API"
.SH NAME
mu2edaq_commandline_tools \- Python API for the Mu2e DAQ command line tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B from mu2edaq_commandline_tools import config, cli, __version__
.PP
.BI "config.load(" "explicit_path=None, overrides=None, environ=None" ") -> dict"
.BI "config.find_config(" "explicit=None" ") -> pathlib.Path | None"
.BI "config.config_search_paths(" "explicit=None" ") -> Iterable[Path]"
.BI "config.load_file(" path ") -> dict"
.BI "config.load_env(" "environ=None" ") -> dict"
.BI "config.DEFAULTS" "  # dict of built-in defaults"
.PP
.BI "cli.main(" "argv=None" ") -> int"
.BI "cli.COMMANDS" "  # dict of name -> Command(help, add_arguments, run)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS Configuration
.B config.load()
builds the effective configuration by applying, in increasing order of
precedence: the built-in
.BR DEFAULTS ,
the YAML config file, the
.BR MU2EDAQ_TOOLS_ *
environment, and
.IR overrides .
.PP
.I overrides
carries the parsed command line. Entries whose value is
.B None
are ignored, so an argparse option the user did not give does not clobber a
lower-precedence source. Keys not present in
.B DEFAULTS
are ignored from every source.
.PP
The returned dict holds one extra key,
.BR _config_file ,
naming the file that was read, or
.B None
when none was found. Keys beginning with an underscore are metadata, not
settings.
.PP
.B load()
raises
.B ValueError
when the YAML file's top level is not a mapping,
.B OSError
when it cannot be read, and
.B RuntimeError
when PyYAML is missing. A config file that simply does not exist is not an
error.
.PP
.I environ
defaults to
.BR os.environ ;
pass a dict to make tests hermetic.
.SS Adding a tool
Register a subcommand in
.BR cli.COMMANDS .
A
.B Command
is a named tuple of a help string, an optional hook that adds argparse
options to the subparser, and a run hook called as
.RI run( args ", " cfg )
that returns a process exit status:
.PP
.nf
.RS
from mu2edaq_commandline_tools.cli import COMMANDS, Command

def _add_ping(parser):
    parser.add_argument("host")

def cmd_ping(args, cfg):
    print("pinging %s on %s" % (args.host, cfg["cluster"]))
    return 0

COMMANDS["ping"] = Command("check a node responds", _add_ping, cmd_ping)
.RE
.fi
.PP
The tool then appears in
.BR "mu2edaq-tools list" ,
gets the global options and config resolution for free, and needs no changes
to the dispatcher.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS
from mu2edaq_commandline_tools import config

cfg = config.load(overrides={"cluster": args.cluster})
print(cfg["cluster"], "from", cfg["_config_file"] or "defaults")
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2edaq-tools (1),
.BR mu2edaq_cli_core (3)
